1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome back. Hooks are where you stop your agent from doing damage. Get this wrong, and a model can

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
run a destructive command before anyone reviews it. The fix is a guardrail that fires before the

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
action, not a line in a prompt that you hope it obeys. This lesson covers how hooks block

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
dangerous tool calls. Let's dig in. Let's start with three terms that anchor everything here.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:32,000
First, hooks. These are deterministic scripts you register in settings.json, and they fire at fixed

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
points in the agent's lifecycle, completely outside the model. Second, hooks as guardrails.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
That's the specific move of using a hook to deny or ask on a dangerous pending action before it

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
executes, which gives you enforcement that doesn't depend on what the model decided.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Third, least privilege. That means granting a tool or identity only the capabilities it

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
truly needs, and removing everything else, so a hijacked agent simply has less it can reach.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Keep these three in mind, because the rest of the lesson builds directly on them.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Here's the idea that makes hooks powerful. A hook is code you register, not guidance you hope the

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
model follows. It lives in settings.json, and it fires at a fixed lifecycle event, like the moment

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
before a tool runs. Because it's real code, it runs the same way every single time, no matter

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
what the model produced in that turn. That's what we mean by deterministic. The behavior is

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
reproducible, and you can reason about it with confidence. Compare that to a sentence in your

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
system prompt asking the model to be careful. It might follow it, or it might not, depending on the

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,000
input. A hook removes that uncertainty. It's enforcement, and enforcement is exactly what a

19
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,000
guardrail is supposed to be. This next distinction is the one people get wrong, so let's be precise.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:02,000
There are two tool events, and timing is everything. Pretool use fires before a tool call executes.

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Because nothing has happened yet, it can deny the call, ask the user, or even rewrite the pending

22
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
arguments. That's the guardrail point. PostToolUse fires after the call has already run. It can log

23
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
what happened, check the result, or trigger a follow-up. But it can't prevent anything, because

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
the action is already done, so if you're trying to stop a destructive command, PostToolUse is too late

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
by definition. The file is already deleted. Whenever the goal is prevention, you reach for

26
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,000
PretoolUse and only PretoolUse. Let's stay on PretoolUse, because it's the event you'll register

27
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
most often for safety. It fires before the tool call runs, which is the whole reason blocking

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
is still on the table. There's one more thing that makes it special. PretoolUse is the only tool

29
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
event that can rewrite or sanitize a pending call's arguments before execution. So it can not only

30
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
block a bad command, it can also fix one, swapping in a safer version. The lesson here is about

31
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
choosing your lifecycle event deliberately. If you wire your safety check to an after-the-fact event,

32
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
your guardrail shows up after the damage is done. Pick the event that fires before the action, and

33
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
your check actually has the power to stop it. Now let's look at the four values a PretoolUse hook

34
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,000
can return, because this exact set matters. The field is called PermissionDecision, and it takes

35
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
four values. The first is Allow, which permits the pending call to proceed as requested. The second

36
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:43,000
is Deny, which blocks the call outright, stopping the action before it runs. The third is Ask, which

37
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
escalates to the user with a permission dialog, so a human approves it. And the fourth is Defer,

38
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,000
which hands the decision back to the normal permission flow. Watch out for the common trap

39
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:01,000
here. People remember Allow, Deny, and Ask, and forget Defer. There are four, not three, and Defer

40
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
is the one that slips people up. Let's make this concrete with the classic example, blocking a

41
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,000
destructive shell command. Picture your agent about to run something like rm-rf on an important

42
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
directory. First, a matcher narrows the hook so it only triggers on the bash tool, so you're not

43
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
inspecting every action. Then the hook looks at the actual command and checks it against patterns

44
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
you care about, like a recursive force delete. If it matches, the hook returns Deny with a clear

45
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,000
reason that gets shown back, explaining why it was blocked. There's also a shortcut, a hook that

46
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
simply exits with code two blocks the call and passes its message along as the error. Either way,

47
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
the command is stopped before a single file is deleted. Let's tie together three ideas about why

48
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
this approach is trustworthy. First, permission decision. The field, a pre-tool use hook, sets to

49
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:06,000
control a pending call with its four values, Allow, Deny, Ask, and Defer. Second, deterministic

50
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
enforcement. The hook fires and decides the same way on every run. Unlike a prompt instruction, the

51
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
model can interpret loosely or ignore under pressure. That reliability is the entire point.

52
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Third, the exit code path. Instead of returning JSON, a hook can exit with code two to block the

53
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
call and return its message as the error, which is a compact way to enforce a rule. Put these

54
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
together and you have policy that holds regardless of how the model reasons that turn.

55
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
One last principle, and it's about not relying on any single control. A hook that denies an action

56
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:50,000
is good, but it's even stronger when the dangerous capability isn't there in the first place. That's

57
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:57,000
least privilege. You remove access the agent doesn't need, so there's less it can even attempt. Now the

58
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,000
hook becomes a second layer rather than your only defense. Think about it this way. If a capability

59
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:09,000
is both absent by design and blocked by a hook when something tries to reach for it, an attacker

60
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:16,000
has two independent barriers to get past. That's real defense in depth. Deterministic hooks layered

61
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
over a least privilege baseline is the posture you want for anything that can do damage.

62
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Here's the bottom line. Hooks give you deterministic enforcement outside the model.

63
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Pre-tool use is the guardrail because it fires before the action, while post-tool use runs after

64
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:40,000
and can't prevent anything. It only logs. Just remember, permission decision has four values,

65
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:48,000
allow, deny, ask, and defer. That covers blocking destructive actions with hooks. I'll see you in the next one.

